  "query_preview": "..High pressure is centered over south central\nArizona. Ample moisture is in place with PWATs 25-50 percent \nabove normal and dewpoints generally in the low 60s. However \neven with the moisture in place the proximity of the high will \nkeep storm activity subdued. CAMs show most storm activity on \nthe southern periphery of the high over Sonora with isolated \nstorms along the international border south of Tucson. Isolated \nstorms will also be possible over the White Mountains where \nthere are already a few buildups. Instability and surface \nheating won't be enough to overcome the synoptic scale \nsubsidence for most of the CWA. Storms that do form will be \ncapable of locally heavy rainfall, which will be the primary \nthreat. \n\nFriday the high nudges a little further to the east over the\nbootheel of New Mexico. This will allow for a little more\nactivity compared to today. CAMs show isolated to scattered\nconvection during the afternoon and evening south and east of \nTucson. Heavy rainfall will again be the primary threat with a\nmore isolated strong wind threat. The 10/12Z HREF paints Santa\nCruz County and southern Cochise County as having the best\nchances for heavy rainfall. Probabilities for one inch hourly\nrainfall rates are 20-60 percent with the highest probabilities\nin Santa Cruz County. Two inch hourly rainfall rates aren't out\nof the question either with probabilities 10-25 percent in Santa\nCruz County Friday afternoon and evening. Expect this heavy\nrainfall to lead to isolated flash flooding and rises in area\nwashes and rivers. \n\nAbove normal moisture sticks around through at least next\nweekend, if not beyond according to both GFS and Euro ensembles.\nThis weekend the ridge slowly retreats to the east as a closed low\nmoves through the Pacific Northwest and into the northern\nRockies. By Sunday the center of the high is over the\nsoutheast U.S., and stays in that relative position into the \nmiddle of next week. With moisture not really decreasing expect \ncontinued daily afternoon and evening thunderstorm activity. \nThis weekend storm chances will likely be most focused south and\neast of Tucson. Early next week with the high retreating \nfurther east NBM PoPs increase and also expand westward with \n20-50 percent PoPs from central Pima County eastward.",
